The triangle/parallelogram rule provides a geometric interpretation
of vector addition.

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% SCALAR MULTIPLICATION
% =================================================

\begin{frame}{Scalar Multiplication}

Scalar multiplication means multiplying every component of a
vector by the same number.

\[
k\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
kv_1\\
kv_2
\end{bmatrix}
\]

\textbf{Example:}

Let

\[
\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

and $k=3$.

Then

\[
3\mathbf v
=
3
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
6\\
3
\end{bmatrix}
\]

The direction remains the same while the magnitude becomes
three times larger.

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% LINEAR COMBINATION
% =================================================

\section{Linear Combinations, Span and Basis}

\begin{frame}{Linear Combination}

A linear combination is an expression formed by multiplying
vectors by scalars and adding the results.

\[
\mathbf w
=
a\mathbf v_1+b\mathbf v_2
\]

where $a$ and $b$ are scalars.

\textbf{Example:}

Let

\[
\mathbf v_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf v_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Then

\[
2\mathbf v_1+3\mathbf v_2
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
3
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Therefore, $(2,3)$ is a linear combination of the two basis
vectors.

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% SPAN
% =================================================

\begin{frame}{Span}

The \textbf{span} of a set of vectors is the collection of all
vectors that can be created using their linear combinations.

For example,

\[
\mathbf e_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf e_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Their span is

\[
\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf e_1,\mathbf e_2\}
=
\mathbb R^2
\]

because every vector

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y
\end{bmatrix}
=
x\mathbf e_1+y\mathbf e_2
\]

can be generated.

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% BASIS
% =================================================

\begin{frame}{Basis Vectors}

A basis is a set of vectors that:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item spans the space, and
    \item is linearly independent.
\end{enumerate}

For $\mathbb R^2$, the standard basis is

\[
\mathbf e_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf e_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Every vector in $\mathbb R^2$ can be written as

\[
\mathbf v=x\mathbf e_1+y\mathbf e_2.
\]

\end{frame}

