\section{Change of Basis}

\begin{frame}{Change of Basis}

A basis provides a coordinate system for representing vectors.

The standard basis of $\mathbb R^2$ is

\[
\mathbf e_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf e_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\1
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

Suppose another basis is

\[
\mathbf b_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf b_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\-1
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

The same vector can have different coordinates depending
on the chosen basis.

\end{frame}

% -------------------------------------------------

\begin{frame}{Change of Basis Graph}

\begin{center}

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]

\draw[->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

\draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(2,0)
node[below]{$\mathbf e_1$};

\draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(0,2)
node[left]{$\mathbf e_2$};

\draw[->,very thick] (0,0)--(2,2)
node[above]{$\mathbf b_1$};

\draw[->,very thick] (0,0)--(2,-2)
node[below]{$\mathbf b_2$};

\end{tikzpicture}

\end{center}

Different bases describe the same vector space using different
coordinate directions.

\end{frame}

