\section{Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues}

\begin{frame}{Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues}

An eigenvector of a matrix is a non-zero vector whose direction
does not change after the transformation.

It satisfies

\[
\boxed{
A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v
}
\]

where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf v$ is the eigenvector.
    \item $\lambda$ is the eigenvalue.
    \item $A$ is the transformation matrix.
\end{itemize}

The eigenvalue tells us how much the eigenvector is stretched
or compressed.

\end{frame}

% -------------------------------------------------

\begin{frame}{Eigenvalue Example}

Let

\[
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
2&0\\
0&3
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

Consider

\[
\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

Then

\[
A\mathbf v
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
=
2
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{\lambda=2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}}
\]

is an eigenvector.

\end{frame}

% -------------------------------------------------

\begin{frame}{Eigenvector Graph}

\begin{center}

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]

\draw[->] (-0.5,0)--(4,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

\draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(1.5,0)
node[above]{$\mathbf v$};

\draw[->,very thick] (0,0)--(3,0)
node[above]{$A\mathbf v=2\mathbf v$};

\end{tikzpicture}

\end{center}

The direction remains unchanged; only the length changes.

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% VECTOR SPACES
% =================================================

\section{Vector Spaces}

\begin{frame}{Vector Space}

A vector space is a collection of objects called vectors that
can be added together and multiplied by scalars while satisfying
certain rules.

Examples include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbb R^2$
    \item $\mathbb R^3$
    \item Polynomial spaces
    \item Matrix spaces
    \item Function spaces
\end{itemize}

For example,

\[
\mathbb R^2=
\left\{
(x,y):x,y\in\mathbb R
\right\}.
\]

\end{frame}

% =================================================
% SUBSPACE
% =================================================

\begin{frame}{Subspace}

A subspace is a subset of a vector space that is itself a
vector space.

For a subset $W$ to be a subspace:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathbf0\in W$
    \item It must be closed under addition.
    \item It must be closed under scalar multiplication.
\end{enumerate}

For example, a line passing through the origin is a subspace
of $\mathbb R^2$.

